  "why": "The statement asks only for two primes summing to every even N > 2. The Liouville–Goldbach work answers a different question: the summands need only have λ = −1, and the result is implied by Goldbach but does not imply it. So both repositories, the MathOverflow answer, the announcement and Mangerel's two papers are variants. Their AI attribution (GPT-6 Astra, 'generated' on the announcer's reported word, though disputed) has no bearing on this problem. Computational extensions are instances, and Chen-type, ternary, twin-prime and odd-range results are variants. Each whole-problem proof claim (Preprints.org, SSRN, marginal journals, academia.edu, old arXiv claims) shows several F10 signals together: it claims a landmark, nobody took it up, some sit in marginal venues or have had many revisions, and the field still calls the problem open. I marked these not_credible. Abdelqader's DeepSeek R1 credit reads as assisted on its own quote, but the event is not_credible. The formal-conjectures claim is knocked_down by the identified false lemma. Close call on dates: preprints 202410.1262 is dated year-only, but its manuscript ID places it in October 2024, so I answered after_snapshot true. Gurgel 2024 has no such anchor, so it is null.",
